Today, I learned about AWS Lambda, a serverless compute service that allows us to run code without managing servers.

What I Learned

* What AWS Lambda is and why it is used.
* How Lambda follows a serverless architecture.
* How a Lambda function is executed in response to an event/trigger.
* Lambda supports languages such as Python, Java, JavaScript/Node.js, Go, and more.
* Understanding the basic structure of a Lambda function, including the handler.
* How Lambda automatically handles scaling based on incoming requests.
* Lambda uses a pay-per-use model, so we generally pay based on the compute time and requests used.
* Learned about common Lambda triggers such as API Gateway, S3, EventBridge, SQS, SNS, and CloudWatch.
* Understood the difference between running an application on EC2 and using Lambda for event-driven workloads.
* Learned about important Lambda concepts such as execution roles, environment variables, timeout, memory, concurrency, and CloudWatch Logs.
* Understood how Lambda can be used for automation and scheduled tasks, such as cleaning unused AWS resources.

Simple Example

For example, if an object is uploaded to an S3 bucket, that event can trigger a Lambda function automatically. The function can then process the file without requiring us to keep an EC2 server running continuously.

Key Takeaway

The main thing I learned today is that AWS Lambda lets us focus on writing and running code while AWS manages the underlying servers, scaling, and infrastructure.
